1. Workflow Standardization: (to create a repetable checklist so every dataset gets the same structured treatment)
    i. Step-by-step EDA checklist 
        a. Dataset overview:
            Load dataset (pd.read_csv, pd.read_excel, etc)
            Inspect shape (df.shape) -> rows x columns
            Preview df.head(), df.describe()

        b. Missing values
            Count missing: df.isnull().sum()
            Percentage missing per column 
            Decide strategy: drop, impute, or flag

        c. Data types and Structure 
            Identify numeric, categorical, text, datetime columns 
            Convert types if needed (pd.to_datetime, astype)
            Check unique values for categorical features

        d. Univariate Analysis
            Numeric: histograms, boxplots, summary stats 
            Categorical: value counts, bar plots 
            Text: word counts, token frequency 

        e. Bivariate Analysis
            Numeric vs Numeric: scatterplots, correlation 
            Numeric vs Categorical: boxplots, group means 
            Categorical vs Categorical: cross-tabulations, stacked bar plots 

        f. Multivariate Analysis 
            Correlation matrix and heatmaps
            PCA for dimensionality reduction
            Clustering (optional)

        g. Outlier detection
            Boxplots, z-scores, IQR method
            Decide whether to cap, transform, or drop 

        h. feature engineering (if needed)
            Create interactive terms
            Encode categorical variables
            Scale/normalize numeric features 

        i. Documentation
            Record key findings
            Save plots and summaries 
            Build reproducible notebook/scripts

    ii. Handling large datasets
        a. Use df.sample(1000) for quick previews
        b. Profile subsets before scaling up
        c. Consider efficient libraries like Dask, Vaex, or Polars for distributed EDA 
        d. Use effienent plotting (eg hexbin plots, KDE instead of scatter for millions of points)

2. Automation Tools (to build helper functions that automate repetitive tasks)
    i. Helper fuctions:
        a. missing_summary(df) -> % missing per column
        b. outlier_summary(df) -> outlier counts per numeric column 
        c. category_report(df) -> frequency tables for categorical variables
    ii. Mini EDA report builders
        a. Combine summaries + plots into a single function 
            eg: eda_report(df) -> prints dataset info, missing summary, top correlatioins, and key plots.
            can be extended to jupyter notebooks or dashboards

3. Time-Series EDA (Extend EDA skills to temporal datasets)
    i. Rolling statistics
        a. Moving averages, rolling mean/variance for trend detection
            eg: df['value'].rolling(window=7).mean() for weekly smoothing
    ii. Seasonal decomposition
        a. Use statsmodels.tsa.seasonal_decompose() to split series into trend, seasonality, and residuals
        b. Helps identify cyclical patterns (eg hospital admissions, sales)

4. Time-Series EDA 
    1. Rolling Statistics 
        Smooth out short-term fluctuations and highlight longer-term trends
        a. Rolling Mean / Moving Average 
        
            df['rolling_mean_7'] = df['value'].rolling(window=7).mean()

        b. Rolling Variance / Std Dev (to detect volatility)

            df['rolling_std_7'] = df['value'].rolling(window=7).std()